<div class="content-page">
  <h1>About</h1>
  <p>
    I'm a potter working out of a community studio, where I make functional pieces like teapots,
    cups and bowls alongside more sculptural forms under the name Bremmer Artistry. I started about
    three years ago, when a friend gave me a free class, and I've kept coming back to the wheel ever
    since.
  </p>
  <p>
    Almost everything I make begins on the wheel, and I leave the throwing lines visible because I
    like the calm of a thrown form. For my Lightning-Cracked Collection, I wait until the pot is
    leather-hard and carve branching channels into the wall by hand, letting them fork from rim to
    foot without planning the path. Glaze pools in the cuts and breaks over their edges, so the lines
    fire to a different color and depth than the body.
  </p>
  <p>
    For my Peeled Collection, I wrap a thrown form in a slab of clay, then tear that outer skin open
    and fold it back like a husk splitting around a seed. The smooth form underneath gets a
    contrasting glaze, so each piece shows both its shell and what the shell was holding.
  </p>
  <p>
    I'm drawn to the marks natural forces leave behind: lightning across a sky, rivers splitting into
    a delta, bark lifting from a trunk. I want my pots to stay useful while keeping that kind of
    tension visible.
  </p>
  <p>
    When you pick up one of my pieces, I hope you can see where the wheel left off and the carving or
    tearing began. Thank you for taking the time to look.
  </p>

